{% block titre %}Viderunt omnes{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Viderunt omnes, Christmas gradual: the perfect example of the melisma, those long garlands of notes on a single syllable.{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>Example 2 — <span class="mot-latin">Viderunt omnes</span></h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    A gradual for Christmas Day, and one of the summits of Gregorian art&nbsp;: here a
    single syllable can carry dozens of notes. Welcome to the world of the
    <strong>melisma</strong>.
</p>

<div class="fiche">
    <dl>
        <dt>Genre</dt><dd>Gradual (meditative chant, after the 1st reading)</dd>
        <dt>Occasion</dt><dd>Mass of Christmas Day</dd>
        <dt>Text</dt><dd>Psalm 97 (98), verses 3 and 4, then verse 2</dd>
        <dt>Mode</dt><dd>Mode V (<em>fa</em> mode, solemn and radiant)</dd>
        <dt>Style</dt><dd><strong>Melismatic</strong>&nbsp;: long garlands of notes per syllable</dd>
        <dt>In the <em>Triplex</em></dt><dd>p. 48</dd>
    </dl>
</div>

<div class="lecteur-audio">
    <span class="etiquette">Listen to the chant</span>
    <p class="note-audio">Listen to how a single syllable stretches into a long vocalise, depending on the performer.</p>
