<h2>The text and its translation</h2>
<div class="texte-latin">
    <div class="entete lat">Latin</div>
    <div class="entete fr">English</div>
    <div class="lat">Viderunt omnes fines terræ<br>salutare Dei nostri&nbsp;:</div>
    <div class="fr">All the ends of the earth have seen<br>the salvation of our God&nbsp;;</div>
    <div class="lat">jubilate Deo, omnis terra.</div>
    <div class="fr">acclaim God, all the earth.</div>
    <div class="lat"><span class="chip">Verse</span> Notum fecit Dominus salutare suum&nbsp;:<br>ante conspectum gentium revelavit justitiam suam.</div>
    <div class="fr">The Lord has made known his salvation&nbsp;;<br>in the sight of the nations he has revealed his justice.</div>
</div>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — Melisma</span>
    <p>
        A <strong>melisma</strong> is a group of many notes sung on a single syllable
        (sometimes twenty, thirty notes or more&nbsp;!). It is the opposite of
        «&nbsp;syllabic&nbsp;» chant (one note per syllable). The melisma is the voice
        <em>rejoicing</em>, drawing out a vowel as one draws out a joy.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Why so many notes&nbsp;?</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    A gradual is not made to be understood «&nbsp;word by word&nbsp;» like a reading&nbsp;:
    it is a chant of contemplation, entrusted to a soloist or a small, highly trained group.
    On key syllables — for instance the last one of <span class="mot-latin">om-nes</span> or
    of <span class="mot-latin">ter-ræ</span> — the melody blossoms into long vocalises. So
    do not try to «&nbsp;read fast&nbsp;»&nbsp;: here, one savours.
</p>

<h3>How to read a long melisma without getting lost</h3>
<p>
    Faced with a garland of notes, the key is to <strong>cut it up into familiar little
    neumes</strong>. A melisma is never more than a sequence of bricks you already
    know&nbsp;:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
<strong>climacus</strong> figures (a note then a descending cascade of diamonds) — very
frequent in descents&nbsp;;
</li>
    <li>
linked <strong>pes</strong> and <strong>clivis</strong> figures, which make the line
undulate&nbsp;;
</li>
    <li>
sometimes a <strong>quilisma</strong>, that zigzag note which serves as a springboard in the
middle of an ascent.
</li>
</ul>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">The role of the old notations here</span>
    <p>
        In such a dense melisma, the square notation in the middle shows clearly
        <em>which</em> notes follow one another. But it is above all the layers of
        <strong>Laon</strong> and <strong>St. Gall</strong> that rescue the performer&nbsp;:
        they show where to <em>speed up</em> (letter <strong>c</strong>, <span
        class="mot-latin">celeriter</span>), where to <em>settle</em> (letter
        <strong>t</strong>, <span class="mot-latin">tenete</span>, or an episema), so that
        the vocalise breathes instead of turning into a mush of notes.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Did you know — the jubilus</span>
    <p>
        The great melisma of joy is sometimes called the <span
        class="mot-latin">jubilus</span>, typically the one that blossoms on the final
        «&nbsp;a&nbsp;» of <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia</span>. Saint Augustine
        described it as the song of a heart too full to find its words. <span
        class="mot-latin">Viderunt omnes</span> belongs to that same family of exuberant
        writing.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Seeing the three notations</h2>
<figure class="facsimile">
    <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/viderunt-omnes.jpg') }}" alt="Viderunt omnes in the Graduale Triplex, with the melisma on -nes">
    <figcaption>The gradual "Viderunt omnes", with its long melisma on "-nes" of "omnes", in the <em>Graduale Triplex</em> (Solesmes, 1979), p.&nbsp;48.</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="encadre">
    <span class="etiquette">Key point for this chant</span>
    <p>
        <span class="mot-latin">Viderunt omnes</span> illustrates the
        <strong>melismatic</strong> style. The right reflex&nbsp;: <strong>cut up</strong>
        the long vocalises into familiar neumes, and rely on the significative letters for
        the rhythm. After this feast of notes, let us return to a more intimate chant&nbsp;:
        the dawn of Christmas.
    </p>
</div>

